WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.950
Over the years, DC's artists and writers have
told all kinds of Batman stories - and Batman

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.974
himself often changes along the way.

00:00:06.974 --> 00:00:11.668
But there are a few key aspects of the character
that always stay the same… almost always, at least.

00:00:11.668 --> 00:00:14.203
These are the rules Batman has to follow.

00:00:14.203 --> 00:00:18.590
It's hard to pinpoint exactly when, but at
some point during the last 80 years, the silhouette

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:24.468
of the bat became more closely associated
with a DC superhero franchise than, well, actual bats.

00:00:24.468 --> 00:00:28.730
The basic elements of the Batman costume are
essential to his identity in ways that don't

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:31.334
necessarily apply to other fictional characters.

00:00:31.334 --> 00:00:35.250
For instance, Hugh Jackman plays Wolverine
in multiple X-Men movies but doesn't wear

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.640
his iconic mask once, and that works just
fine — because as long as you can see Wolverine's

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.629
claws, you know he's Wolverine.

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:46.401
Additionally, the attitude and mannerisms
define James Bond more than any specific visual cues.

00:00:46.401 --> 00:00:49.880
But Batman needs his Batsuit, and a big part
of that is the insignia.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.780
Now, there have been a few occasions on which
Batman has foregone the insignia - his Insider

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.534
suit doesn't have it, for example, and certain
alternate versions of the character don't use it, either.

00:00:58.534 --> 00:01:02.579
But these are rare exceptions that prove a
very important rule: namely, that the big

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:06.701
bad bat symbol is as much a part of the character
as Alfred or the Batmobile.

00:01:06.701 --> 00:01:09.901
Get rid of it, and you might as well be reading
Daredevil.

00:01:09.901 --> 00:01:13.360
As Batman knows, a good cape can come in very
handy every now and again.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.049
Beyond the cape's advantages in hand-to-hand
combat, however, there's also the fact that

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:21.130
it's a crucial part of looking badass standing
on a tall building in Gotham - and we all

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:23.330
know how much Batman loves to do that.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.310
Still, he doesn't always wear a cape.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:28.840
The Terry McGinnis version of the character
doesn't wear one, while the Future State version

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.468
of Bruce Wayne goes without, too, as does
that pesky Insider suit.

00:01:32.468 --> 00:01:36.280
But even if he's not sporting a cape, there's
one thing the character should always have:

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.587
a mask with little, pointy, decorative ears.

00:01:38.587 --> 00:01:42.360
Of course, the size and shape of Batman's
ears have varied over the years - heck, they're

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.493
barely even visible on Affleck's suit - but
they're always there.

00:01:45.493 --> 00:01:49.340
And while they may not be quite as iconic
as the cape or even the insignia, Batman's

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.070
ears have become a staple of the hero's design
- and they've even gone on to influence a

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:59.334
handful of other superfolk over the years,
including Catwoman, Catman, Daredevil, and Black Panther.

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:04.040
From the unmistakably snazzy effects of the
campy 1960s series to Robert Pattinson just

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.759
straight-up laying into a street thug in The
Batman, it's clear that Bruce Wayne has always

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:09.278
been a punchy kinda guy.

00:02:09.278 --> 00:02:13.060
There are a few good reasons why this has
become such a crucial part of the character.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.858
For one, there's a basic, primal thrill to
be found in it.

00:02:15.858 --> 00:02:19.469
Even though fighting isn't all that fun in
real life, fictional combat is exhilarating

00:02:19.469 --> 00:02:22.749
like almost nothing else; why else do action
movies even exist?

00:02:22.749 --> 00:02:26.650
So any writer who needs to inject a little
excitement into their Batman story simply

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.409
needs to provide Gotham's Guardian with a
face to punch, a stomach to kick, or a limb

00:02:30.409 --> 00:02:33.110
to snap, and voila — instant entertainment.

00:02:33.276 --> 00:02:35.609
"You ever dance with the devil in the pale
moonlight?"

00:02:39.528 --> 00:02:43.419
From Batman's in-story perspective, criminals
might not find him so intimidating if they

00:02:43.419 --> 00:02:45.650
knew they stood a chance against him in a
fistfight.

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:49.849
So his reputation as a master of several martial
arts disciplines makes it easier to intimidate

00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:54.279
potential foes… and also allows him to solve
the majority of his problems with a well-placed

00:02:54.279 --> 00:02:56.000
knock to a skull or ten.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.840
Some of Batman's adversaries might be too
powerful to beat up, of course, while others

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.309
might be smart enough to avoid a punching
contest with him.

00:03:02.309 --> 00:03:06.650
But for your typical everyday purse snatcher-type,
a haymaker wrapped in a Bat-glove will always

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:08.802
do the trick - and then some.

00:03:08.802 --> 00:03:12.079
They don't call him the World's Greatest Detective
for nothing, you know.

00:03:12.079 --> 00:03:15.909
Batman might be able to throw a punch like
nobody else, but he's just not Batman if he

00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:18.541
doesn't get the chance to flex his strongest
muscle of all.

00:03:18.541 --> 00:03:22.150
When Bruce Wayne first devoted himself to
warring against crime, he didn't just spend

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:26.540
the following years honing his fighting prowess
to virtually unmatched levels - he also developed

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:30.234
one of the finest forensic and scientific
minds Gotham has ever known.

00:03:30.234 --> 00:03:33.920
Particularly when dealing with foes such as
the Riddler and the Joker, it's important

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.101
to be able to decipher the clues they usually
leave lying around.

00:03:37.101 --> 00:03:40.889
Batman's powers of detection also come in
very handy when hunting the hordes of criminals

00:03:40.889 --> 00:03:42.709
who don't actually want to be caught, too.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:47.140
And sure, not every version of Batman has
the genius-level deduction skills or access

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.569
to the lab equipment that the billionaire
Bruce Wayne enjoys.

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:53.430
Nonetheless, they all must keep their perception
sharp and their thinking sophisticated, ready

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:57.515
for the next time they run into a dilemma
they can't get out of with a punch and a kick.

00:03:57.515 --> 00:04:01.579
In the Marvel Universe, superheroes usually
live in fictionalized versions of real-world

00:04:01.579 --> 00:04:03.943
cities such as New York and San Francisco.

00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:09.030
In the DC Universe, the action does sometimes
take place in or around real-world locations,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:12.977
but most stories occur in made-up municipalities
like Metropolis or Gotham City.

00:04:12.977 --> 00:04:17.191
These places play host to a wide variety of
criminals and crime-fighters - but while other

00:04:17.191 --> 00:04:20.950
heroes tend to bounce around the map, Batman
always resides in Gotham.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.510
The movies often copy and paste multiple urban
locations into an amalgam they can use as

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:29.660
Gotham, with parts of New York, London, Tokyo,
Chicago, and numerous other towns standing

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.010
in for the city in various Batman movies.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.470
So while Gotham looks very distinctive in
some renderings, it's more often depicted

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:39.334
as a fictionalized version of every American
city in the Midwest and the Northeast.

00:04:39.334 --> 00:04:43.690
And while it may not have the glitz and glamor
of Los Angeles or the chic East Coast flair

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:47.430
of New York, it does at least seem like the
kind of place a feller could open a costume

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.101
store and do alright for himself.

00:04:49.101 --> 00:04:54.525
Maybe that's why Batman never seems to want
to leave it behind - that guy just can't resist a good freak.

00:04:54.525 --> 00:04:58.910
At the end of the day, most Batman stories
come about because companies like DC and Warner

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:02.850
Bros. want to make money - and one way of
doing that is by mass-producing toys, action

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.460
figures, and other such merchandise.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:09.489
Certainly, this partly explains why Batman
always drives a customized car or similar Bat-vehicle.

00:05:09.489 --> 00:05:11.740
But let's not get too wrapped up in cynicism,
here.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:16.935
After all, unlike some DC heroes, Batman can't
fly, and he's got to get from place to place somehow.

00:05:16.935 --> 00:05:21.440
Although it's a near-constant feature in Batman's
vast canon, the Batmobile has taken many different

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:22.658
forms over the years.

00:05:22.658 --> 00:05:26.680
The heavy-duty monstrosity from Batman Begins
marked a departure from the sleek, stylish

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:30.550
vehicle on display in Tim Burton's Batman
and Batman Returns, for example.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:34.600
And they both look far less hokey than the
one Adam West drives in the '60s TV show.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:38.430
Indeed, the Batmobile appears to change far
more frequently than Batman himself.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:41.940
While different characters might take up the
bat-mantle every now and then, and the setting

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:46.190
occasionally changes to a different historical
era, Batman's design has still remained far

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:49.080
more consistent than the ever-changing look
of the Batmobile.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:54.204
Batman's definitely not your average joe,
but he does have a few things in common with the rest of us.

00:05:54.204 --> 00:05:56.249
For one, he needs a place to keep his stuff.

00:05:56.249 --> 00:06:00.480
And if he parks the Batmobile out in the driveway
of Wayne Manor, well, there's a decent chance

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.851
that passers-by might put two and two together.

00:06:02.851 --> 00:06:06.810
Traditionally, the Batcave makes a terrific
garage and secret lair, but serves plenty

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.050
of other purposes, too.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:12.310
The Batcave is also a crime lab, a training
gym, a top-of-the-line medical facility, a

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:16.900
supercomputer, a costume-design studio, and
an unofficial trophy room showcasing keepsakes

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:18.670
from Batman's favorite adventures.

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:22.710
Occasionally, it even includes an obstacle
course complete with faux-opponents, similar

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:26.701
to the early version of the Danger Room at
Xavier's School for Gifted Youngsters.

00:06:26.701 --> 00:06:31.490
The Batcave also provides a natural habitat
for a colony of actual bats - because, after

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.095
all, he is called Batman.

00:06:33.594 --> 00:06:36.274
"Charming."

00:06:38.178 --> 00:06:40.860
Bruce Wayne's list of allies is a long one
indeed.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:44.930
It could include: any number of individuals
formerly or currently known as Robin, multiple

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:49.460
crimefighters currently or formerly known
as Batgirl, Catwoman, a few members of the

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:53.434
Gotham City Police Department, Lucius Fox
and a handful of his immediate family members,

00:06:53.434 --> 00:06:56.901
and of course, Bruce Wayne's longtime butler,
Alfred Pennyworth.

00:06:56.901 --> 00:07:00.700
And if a mission requires a trip abroad or
off-planet, he has the rest of the Justice

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:02.738
League to call upon for assistance.

00:07:02.738 --> 00:07:05.570
Even other versions of the character tend
to have friends.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:09.360
For example, Terry McGinnis' primary running
buddy in his future version of Gotham happens

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:10.957
to be Bruce Wayne himself.

00:07:10.957 --> 00:07:15.230
Even the horrifying Batman Who Laughs has
a bunch of creepy vampire-type creatures in

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:16.634
Robin outfits on leashes.

00:07:16.634 --> 00:07:21.280
And sure, in theory, Batman works alone, most
likely so he can tell people that he works

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.000
alone, which is all well and good for sounding
all brooding and dangerous.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.400
But in practice, Batman usually works with
a big ol' heap of pals.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:32.750
To some extent, this is true of every major
superhero - but it's doubly important for

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:36.860
Batman, as, without plenty of personal connections
to keep him grounded, he could end up walking

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:39.108
a very dark path indeed.

00:07:39.108 --> 00:07:43.700
Gadgets are a must-have for any superhero
who doesn't have any actual superpowers, so

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:47.940
it follows that Batman has plenty; heck, he's
even got that iconic utility belt to carry

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:48.935
them all around in.

00:07:48.935 --> 00:07:51.817
"One man's tool is another man's weapon."  

00:07:51.817 --> 00:07:55.273
"In your mind, perhaps there aren't many things you can't turn into a weapon." 

00:07:55.273 --> 00:07:57.604
"Alfred, enough!  the police weren't getting it done."

00:07:57.604 --> 00:08:01.580
Batman's full list of gizmos is pretty much
impossible to count, but one thing that is

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:06.868
certain is that, as far as equipment is concerned,
it's very rare for him to find himself caught short.

00:08:06.868 --> 00:08:10.800
It's worth noting that the exact nature of
Batman's gadgets really depends on the tone

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.660
of the media in which he is appearing.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:16.380
Family-friendly, lighthearted versions of
Batman tend to load their utility belts up

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.500
with unintuitive and even downright silly
accessories.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:21.240
After all, who could forget this?

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:25.502
"Hand me down the shark repellent bat spray!"

00:08:28.625 --> 00:08:32.690
But when it comes to more realistic spins
on Batman's story, he usually goes for grappling

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:35.099
hooks, smoke bombs, and not a whole lot else.

00:08:35.099 --> 00:08:38.829
Occasionally, a Bat-branding iron finds its
way into the mix, but we wouldn't bank on

00:08:38.829 --> 00:08:43.125
that one becoming a regular feature in the
Caped Crusader's arsenal anytime soon.

00:08:43.125 --> 00:08:47.230
It could very well be that the deaths of Thomas
and Martha Wayne have been performed on screen

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:50.449
more than any character who wasn't created
by William Shakespeare.

00:08:50.449 --> 00:08:55.000
But there's more to the loss of Bruce Wayne's
parents than Hollywood's overreliance on franchise fare.

00:08:55.034 --> 00:08:59.140
Simply put: Batman always needs to endure
a soul-annihilating trauma to kickstart his

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:00.769
vendetta against crime.

00:09:00.769 --> 00:09:05.267
In some interpretations, his mission evolves
into a war against suffering and death itself.

00:09:05.267 --> 00:09:09.389
Other times, he just winds up beating up muggers
and serial killers, but either way, if nothing

00:09:09.389 --> 00:09:12.847
bad ever happens to Thomas and Martha, then
Batman doesn't begin.

00:09:12.847 --> 00:09:17.379
Of course, Bruce Wayne isn't the only Batman,
but this rule extends to basically every single

00:09:17.379 --> 00:09:19.629
person who has ever donned the cape and cowl.

00:09:19.629 --> 00:09:25.670
Before they became Bat-heroes, Dick Grayson,
Kate Kane, Thomas Wayne, Jean-Paul Valley,

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:30.459
Damian Wayne, and Terry McGinnis all lost
immediate family members due to acts of senseless,

00:09:30.459 --> 00:09:31.639
often random violence.

00:09:31.639 --> 00:09:35.910
And here lies the true nature of the Caped
Crusader: if all your loved ones are alive

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.620
and well, then you just can't be Batman.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:42.500
The closest you can come is just throwing
on a costume and fighting crime, which, let's

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:44.800
be honest, isn't necessarily the same thing.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:49.480
No matter what shape or form he might take,
Batman is defined by the tragedy of his origin

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:52.449
- and that is the one thing that can never,
ever change.

